---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import PageShell from '../../components/nav/PageShell.astro';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';
import { CRATE_URL, DOCSRS_URL } from '../../consts';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };

const title = 'How QCL works | DeepCausality Quantum';
const description =
  'A QCL program configures a subject, validates it with checks that report what they examined, and controls it with experiments the planner chooses. Failures report what broke.';

const shape = `config(subject, candidates)  →  validate(checks)  →  Screened  →  control(evidence)  →  report`;

const vacuous = `check_markov         Accepted  examined 1  worst margin 0.000e0
check_decomposable   Vacuous   examined 0  worst margin 0.000e0`;

const failure = `[2] Non-commuting model: σx and σz on leg 0
    ✓ validate failed: factors at nodes 0 and 1 do not commute
    the subject is as built: is_frozen() = true

[3] The same non-commuting model on a dynamic graph, through the shipped freeze
    ✓ freeze aborted: factors at nodes 0 and 1 do not commute
    is_frozen() = false (rolled back)`;

/** The composition law for Z̄(0) in the concatenated-code example, one row per precision. */
const law = [
  { precision: 'f32', e1: '1.19 × 10⁻⁶', e2: '4.77 × 10⁻⁷', bound: '2.86 × 10⁻⁶', measured: '9.54 × 10⁻⁷' },
  { precision: 'f64', e1: '2.22 × 10⁻¹⁵', e2: '1.78 × 10⁻¹⁵', bound: '6.22 × 10⁻¹⁵', measured: '1.78 × 10⁻¹⁵' },
  { precision: 'Float106', e1: '0', e2: '0', bound: '0', measured: '0' },
];
---
<PageShell title={title} description={description} path="/how-it-works/">
  <header class="doc-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
    <h1>A QCL program runs in stages.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      QCL, the Quantum Causal Language, is a Rust builder whose programs call <code>config</code>,
      then <code>validate</code>, then <code>control</code>, and the compiler rejects a structural
      candidate that skips <code>validate</code>. Mechanism candidates claim no structure, so a
      plant configured with them goes from <code>config</code> to <code>control</code> directly.
      <code>config</code> sets the subject and its candidates, <code>validate</code> checks them,
      and <code>control</code>, which only a plant reaches, plans experiments and reads their
      results. Every check reports what it examined, and every failure reports what broke.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="shape">
    <h2>The compiler rejects a program that skips the checks.</h2>
    <p>
      For a plant with structural candidates, <code>control</code> takes only the
      <code>Screened</code> value that <code>validate</code> returns: the candidates that passed
      every check, with the report. The plant has no other <code>control</code> implementation, so
      a call that skips <code>validate</code> does not compile.
    </p>
    <Code code={shape} lang="text" themes={SHIKI} wrap />
    <p>
      <code>build()</code> refuses a subject that would answer unsoundly before any stage runs: an
      unfrozen graph, a cyclic causal structure and a plant with no candidates. The stages are steps
      of the causal monad, the crate’s effect type, in a fixed order; QCL defines no new monad.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="subjects">
    <h2>Only a plant runs experiments.</h2>
    <p>
      A plant reaches <code>control</code>, where the planner chooses experiments; a model, a code
      and a circuit stop after <code>validate</code>. Each of the four subjects offers its own
      checks.
    </p>
    <dl class="deflist">
      <div>
        <dt>Plant</dt>
        <dd>
          A system that evolves and is measured, with the observables it exposes and its
          candidates. A structural candidate claims a causal structure and goes through the screen.
          A mechanism candidate claims none and enters control directly. Checks:
          <code>check_markov</code>, <code>check_decomposable</code>. Example:
          <a href="/examples/qcl-crosstalk/">crosstalk</a>.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Model</dt>
        <dd>
          A process written as one Choi–Jamiołkowski operator per node of a frozen graph, meaning a
          graph with fixed node ids that passed the freeze checks. It behaves as a plant with one
          structural candidate and no evidence, and the existing freeze check maps onto it. Checks:
          <code>check_markov</code>, <code>check_decomposable</code>. Example:
          <a href="/examples/qcl-qcm-freeze/">freeze</a>.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Code</dt>
        <dd>
          A chain complex that defines a CSS code, evaluated exactly. It has no experiments, no
          baseline and no evidence, so it offers validate stages only: <code>derive_code</code>,
          <code>check_ldpc_weights</code>, <code>check_class_invariance</code>,
          <code>check_clifford_action</code>. Example:
          <a href="/examples/qcl-geometric-qec/">toric code</a>.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Circuit</dt>
        <dd>
          A compositional circuit model. The library stores the circuit and screens its dilation,
          the circuit rewritten as a factorization the checks can read. Only a circuit can enter an
          abstraction. Checks:
          <code>check_markov</code>, <code>check_decomposable</code>,
          <code>check_alignment_structure</code>, <code>check_naturality</code>. Example:
          <a href="/examples/qcl-crosstalk-circuits/">crosstalk over circuits</a>.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="checks">
    <h2>Every check counts the items it examined.</h2>
    <p>
      Every check returns the quantity it measured, its threshold, the margin (a ratio of the two;
      1 or below accepts), the number of items it examined, and a verdict: Accepted, Rejected or
      Vacuous. Vacuous means that nothing was examined, so nothing was certified.
    </p>
    <p>
      A bare pass would hide that case. On a two-node chain no 3×3 sub-relation exists, because one
      needs three inputs and three outputs. So <code>check_decomposable</code> has nothing to
      search, and the report says so.
    </p>
    <Code code={vacuous} lang="text" themes={SHIKI} wrap />
    <p class="next-links">
      <a href="/checks/">Every check, and what backs it →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="rejection">
    <h2>A rejection drops a candidate, fails the stage, or goes into the report.</h2>
    <p>
      On a plant, <code>check_markov</code> and <code>check_decomposable</code> drop a failing
      candidate from the admitted set. On a model or a circuit, a rejection from those two checks
      fails the stage, and <code>finalize()</code> returns the error; on a circuit,
      <code>check_alignment_structure</code> and <code>check_naturality</code> fail the stage the
      same way. The code checks <code>check_ldpc_weights</code>,
      <code>check_class_invariance</code> and <code>check_clifford_action</code> record the
      rejection in the report, and <code>finalize()</code> returns <code>Ok</code>. A structural
      error, such as a shape mismatch, fails the stage in every case.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="control">
    <h2>Every candidate gets its own copy of the system.</h2>
    <p>
      Control forks one world per candidate, measures or predicts in each world, and adjudicates
      the verdicts. A mechanism plant runs <code>fork</code>, <code>observe</code>,
      <code>gate</code>, <code>adjudicate</code>. A structural plant runs <code>observe</code>,
      <code>fork</code>, <code>predict</code>, <code>compare</code>, <code>adjudicate</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Configured experiments run <code>design_with</code> and <code>fork</code>, then
      <code>observe_experiment</code>, <code>predict_with</code> and <code>compare</code> once per
      experiment, then <code>adjudicate</code>. A <code>Campaign</code> takes a control stage that
      has forked once and repeats the per-experiment loop under
      <code>CausalFlow::iterate_until</code>, stopping when the evidence decides. The stage
      <code>design</code> belongs to no order, because it needs only the candidates and the
      experiments on offer.
    </p>
    <dl class="deflist">
      <div><dt><code>fork</code></dt><dd>Creates one world per candidate, each with its own ledger, and fails if the screen admitted no candidate. A structural candidate’s world holds the plant unchanged; a mechanism candidate’s world holds the plant evolved by that candidate’s channel.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>observe</code></dt><dd>Measures an observable over a number of shots: the plant before the fork, each world’s plant after it.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>predict</code></dt><dd>Evaluates each world’s model for an observable, without spending device time.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>compare</code></dt><dd>Judges each world’s prediction against the observation, in standard errors of the observation.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>gate</code></dt><dd>Judges a read-out against a specification: at least a given value, or at most one.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>design</code></dt><dd>Solves the minimum-cost cover: the cheapest experiments that separate each coverable pair of candidates by a floor of bits. It also lists the pairs no experiment reaches. <code>design_with</code> computes each experiment’s predictions from a response model first.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>observe_experiment</code></dt><dd>Observes a configured experiment from simulated evidence, a lab’s recorded counts, or a published value with its standard error, and records the context it ran in.</dd></div>
      <div><dt><code>adjudicate</code></dt><dd>Folds the worlds’ verdicts and reports a survivor, or the reason the data leaves several candidates standing. A world that read several experiments holds only when every reading agrees, and a pair separates by the sum of its bits over them.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      The ledger counts shots, experiments, predictions, device time, cost and bits. A shot budget
      draws down through checked subtraction, and an overdrawn budget returns an error that carries
      the shortfall.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="failure">
    <h2>An error identifies the nodes, generator or candidate at fault.</h2>
    <p>
      A non-commuting pair (<code>CommutatorNonZero</code>) carries the two node indices as typed
      fields. A Pauli outside the code’s normalizer (<code>NotInNormalizer</code>) carries the index
      of the stabilizer generator it anticommutes with. The triples of a C₃
      (<code>NotFaithfullyRepresentable</code>) and the name of a cyclic candidate
      (<code>CyclicStructureUnsupported</code>) appear in the error’s message.
    </p>
    <p class="next-links">
      <a href={`${CRATE_URL}/src/error/quantum_error.rs`}>Every error variant in the source ↗</a>
      <a href={DOCSRS_URL}>API reference on docs.rs ↗</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="rollback">
    <h2>A failed stage leaves the subject as it was built.</h2>
    <p>
      A stage mutates nothing: after the failed <code>validate</code> below, the model is still
      frozen as built. A failed freeze rolls the graph back to its dynamic state, so a model that
      cannot freeze is never left half frozen.
    </p>
    <Code code={failure} lang="text" themes={SHIKI} wrap />
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="precision">
    <h2>One type alias sets the precision.</h2>
    <p>
      Each example declares <code>type FloatType = Float106;</code>, and the factors, the
      separations, the plan’s costs and every read-out recompute at that precision. Switch it to
      <code>f32</code>, <code>f64</code> or <code>BFloat16</code> and no other line of source
      changes. The results and thresholds move, and some examples state where a narrow type stops
      meeting their tolerance.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>QclBuilder::config</code> fixes two working types once: a real type for accuracy and a
      count type for headroom. The count type moves no threshold. The tolerances form one family
      with five members: commutator, validation, numerical rank, state and shot noise. The first
      four derive from the machine epsilon of the real type, so moving from <code>f64</code> to
      <code>f32</code> moves those thresholds with it. Shot noise derives from the shot count.
    </p>
    <p>
      A hard-coded <code>f64</code> stays invisible while the alias is <code>f64</code> and fails
      to compile the moment the two differ. For that reason the examples default to
      <code>Float106</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="abstractions">
    <h2>QCL measures how far an abstraction is from exact.</h2>
    <p>
      An abstraction relates a detailed model to a coarser one, and QCL reports the distance from
      exact as a residual. Eight physical qubits carry two logical qubits, for example, and the
      logical model describes the same behaviour with fewer parts. Lorenz and Tull
      (arXiv:2602.16612) define when such a map is sound: it must commute with every query in a
      chosen signature, a set of queries. The residual measures the failure to commute.
    </p>
    <p>
      When two abstractions compose, the composition law bounds the composite’s residual:
      <code>ε ≤ ‖τ₂‖_post · ε₁ + ‖τ₁‖_pre · ε₂</code>. Here ε₁ and ε₂ are the two links’
      residuals, and τ₁ and τ₂ are their channel maps. The crate computes both norms for each
      query. In the Frobenius norm on Choi operators neither equals 1 in general, because a partial
      trace over a factor of dimension d has norm √d.
    </p>
    <p>
      The concatenated-code example checks the law for the logical gate Z̄(0) at three precisions.
      The first link’s norm is 1 and the second link’s is 2 at each, so the bound is
      <code>2 · ε₁ + ε₂</code>. At every precision the measured residual is at or under the bound,
      and at <code>Float106</code> both links are exact.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="table-stack law">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Precision</th><th><span class="sym">ε₁</span>, first link</th><th><span class="sym">ε₂</span>, second link</th><th>Bound</th><th>Measured</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {law.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Precision"><code>{r.precision}</code></td>
              <td data-label="ε₁, first link" class="num">{r.e1}</td>
              <td data-label="ε₂, second link" class="num">{r.e2}</td>
              <td data-label="Bound" class="num">{r.bound}</td>
              <td data-label="Measured" class="num">{r.measured}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      When both residuals are zero the composite is exact, and Lean proves that case
      (<code>quantum.abstraction.compose_exact</code>). The bound for nonzero residuals follows
      from the triangle inequality, and Lean has no statement of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A decoder is also an abstraction, from a circuit to its detector error model. The crate
      contains no decoding algorithm; the decoder’s reading of the record enters as data. The
      library injects a fault at each named circuit location and checks the model against the
      circuit. A correlated error that the model omits produces the worst failure, and the failure
      reports the location. Tests cover this path, and no example runs it.
    </p>
    <p class="next-links">
      <a href="/examples/qcl-concatenated-code/">Concatenated code example →</a>
      <a href="/examples/qcl-code-switching/">Code switching example →</a>
      <a href="/examples/qcl-distillation-round/">Distillation example →</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</PageShell>

<style>
  .law { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; }
  .law th, .law td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top; padding: var(--space-3); border-top: 1px solid var(--line-1); }
  .law thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-eyebrow); letter-spacing: var(--ls-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase; font-weight: 400; color: var(--fg-2); border-top: none;
  }
  .law td { color: var(--fg-1); }
  .law td:last-child { color: var(--accent); }
  .law .sym { text-transform: none; }
</style>
